re  quite handily broken down here  they also have commonly used data sets  so there are certain data  sets you see in the fairness community a  lot such as the german credit data set  the compass data set the adult income  data set  which is u.s adult income those are all  in  there quite a large array of fairness  metrics are built  in and what's really nice about this  data set  or code base rather is that they  implement many cutting-edge published  algorithms so unfortunately academics  do not always contribute to one standard  code base often they will just release  the code for their  paper in their own github repository so  you don't  usually have a way of using many  state-of-the-art algorithms in just one  package but that is what aif 360 does  and that's quite nice because of the  sponsorship of ibm  and the fact that it's quite up to date  and just to show you how easy the  example code is to use you can see  here's an example  of a pre-processing tactic so you  pre-process  you train and then you can already  compute a metric just in a few lines  so the struggle here is knowing what  these metrics mean and knowing what  these techniques are  you have to have a conceptual basis to  use them but once you do  the code is already written for you  another example of the iqtas project so  this is out of the center for data  science and public policy at uchicago  although i believe they've moved to  carnegie mellon uh but this  product rather than being about fairness  interventions is about fairness auditing  and they give you either  a web interface a command line interface  or a python library  they have an extensive implementation of  fairness metrics and what they allow you  to do  are reports like this where you will get  a breakdown of the um  the bias they'll tell you sort of what  performance metrics they used what bias  metrics they considered what  groups they considered as far as  sensitive attributes and you will get  visual representations of the bias in  your model so this can be a way to set  up a nice automatic  uh check for bias they also include a  python library so if you want to do your  own coding you can do that as well and i  show you here  just how simple it is and again you get  these automatic representations  so so far i've told you sort of how you  can actively  intervene in your process with aif360  and then iq test is one tool where you  can automate the auditing  here's another one fairml it's a little  bit different iqtas tends to look at  fairness metrics and how they vary by  group  fairml instead looks at feature  importance so they sort of look at the  inputs rather than the output so how are  these  inputs affecting my outputs and seeing  if some of the inputs seem to matter  more than they should  so for example one one example they  provide and again  very easy to use interface um is that if  you look  at i believe this is the compass  algorithm a very famous one they find  that african-american seems to matter a  whole lot but of course it probably  shouldn't right so something like this  showing that an input that shouldn't  matter is in fact highly influential to  the outputs of a model  is another way to look for fairness  problems i notice this  addresses both explainability and  anti-discrimination  and finally there's the ml fairness gym  this comes out of google although it is  unofficial so google will not  necessarily maintain it but what they  introduce is this dynamic rather than  static notion of fairness they  say you can't just look how your model  is doing now what you probably want to  do is simulate  what are the effects of deploying your  model because once you put your model  into effect it produces an action which  in turn influences the environment  but the environment that your algorithm  has acted on  now produces an observation which you  might use for learning  and so they recognize that this care  there could be a cycle where the  algorithm  is both influencing the world but then  influencing itself because it will  continue collecting data  and you can think in this way how might  i have these sort of feedback loops  where i might have runaway bias or  runaway unfairness  or also how can i just think dynamically  about how my model is going to impact  the world  and so what they do is they give you a  simulation environment you think about  sort of fairness metrics you want to use  you think about what kind of agent you  want to use and they have coded up quite  a few  so you can think about college  admissions or classifiers or  allocators and then they let you play  that out over time and they say  okay in this case this is sort of  over time what things are going to look  like so maybe over time things get more  fair or less  fair or distribution gets better or  worse but this is something that has  also been missing from the fairness  literature and is quite useful so that's  another take  um so just to give you an overview after  six of  these state-of-the-art um  state-of-the-art cutting-edge ways to  modify  your training processes or your training  data to enhance fairness  iqritos and fair ml will help you audit  a model  including a black box model and then the  ml fairness gym will help you think  about the dynamic  temporally sensitive aspects of fairness  thinking about how fairness  has to reflect an evolving environment  and shouldn't just be a static view of  your algorithm  so thank you for your time and i look  forward to meeting you at the q a  sessions
 

